## Deployment & Environment

### Environment Variables
- Create `.env` file (never commit)
- Use `.env.example` for template
- Example variables:
  ```
  DATABASE_URL=mysql://user:password@localhost:3306/hotel_booking
  JWT_SECRET=your-secret-key
  NODE_ENV=development
  VITE_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:5000
  VITE_API_TIMEOUT=5000
  ```

### Git Workflow
- Branch naming: `feature/feature-name`, `fix/bug-name`, `hotfix/issue-name`
- Commit messages: descriptive and concise
- Keep commits atomic (one logical change per commit)
- Never commit sensitive data

---

## Documentation Standards

### API Documentation
- Document all endpoints with method, path, parameters, response format
- Include error responses and status codes
- Example request/response payloads

### Code Comments
- Document complex algorithms
- Explain business logic that isn't obvious
- Mark TODO, FIXME, NOTE comments clearly

### README Files
- Project description
- Setup instructions
- Available npm scripts
- Tech stack overview
- Contribution guidelines

---

## Quick Reference Checklist

Before submitting code:
- [ ] Naming conventions followed (snake_case for DB, camelCase for JS, PascalCase for components)
- [ ] Backend: Routes → Controller → Service pattern used
- [ ] Backend: Controllers use explicit exports
- [ ] Backend: Routes import controllers as objects
- [ ] Backend: IDs parsed to integers before DB queries
- [ ] React: Functional components with hooks only
- [ ] React: Async operations have loading/error states
- [ ] No new npm packages installed without permission
- [ ] No hardcoded configuration values
- [ ] No console.log left in production code
- [ ] Proper error handling implemented
- [ ] Code is DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself)
- [ ] Security best practices followed
- [ ] Tests written (if applicable)

